        "“They’re saying rain by the weekend. A lot of it. Coming up out of the south.”",
        "“The bottoms will go first,” he said. He did not mean to say it. It was simply there in his mouth, the way the weight of a load is there in your hands on the wheel. “The road under the railroad, past the elevator. There’s a culvert there that’s too small. It’ll be under before the creek even rises.”",
        "“You always know that kind of thing.”",
        "“It’s the job.” He considered that, and found it was not quite true, and that he did not want to leave it with her untrue. “Thirty-three years of reading water off a windshield. It’s about the only thing I know how to do.”",
        "“That and pay on time.”",
        "“Most months,” he said.",
        "It was nearly a joke. He had not made one in so long that he heard it go out of him with a kind of surprise, the way you hear your own voice on a recording. She heard it too. One corner of her mouth went in.",
        "“Hm,” she said. “Well. You get some sleep. You look like something the cat wouldn’t bother with.” And she went back down the stairs, one at a time, her hand on the rail.",
        "He shut the door and stood with his back to it. The folded paper was in his hand. He put it on the dresser beside the wallet and the keys, and he looked at the three things lying there together, and it seemed to him for a moment that they were the whole of him, a name and a debt and a way into a room, and that a man ought to be more than that, and that once, somewhere, he had been.",
        "* * *",
        "The phone buzzed at a quarter past three. It was the night man, who was also the day man on Mondays, because the yard was short.",
        "“You good for nine?”",
        "“I’m good.”",
        "“North load. Steel. They want it there by daylight.” The night man had a voice like gravel in a coffee can, and he talked to every driver the same way, short, as if each word cost him a little. “Weather’s coming up behind you, but you’ll be ahead of it if you don’t screw around. Take the highway, not the river road. River road’ll be soup by Friday.”",
        "“It’ll be soup by Thursday night,” he said. “The low crossing at the bend goes first. Then the stretch by the stockyard.”",
        "There was a pause on the line. “Yeah,” the night man said. “Probably right. You always are about that.” Another pause. “You okay? You sound like you’re talking from the bottom of a well.”",
