        "“Nine o’clock,” he said, and the night man hung up.",
        "He sat on the edge of the bed with the phone in his hand. He sat that way a long time. The fan went around. The light on the floor moved an inch and then another inch. He thought about the load, the steel, the highway north with the weather coming up behind it like a dog that had not decided yet whether to bite. He thought about it the way he had thought about ten thousand loads, plainly and carefully, and none of it reached him. It was like reading the directions on a box for something he had already thrown away.",
        "At four o’clock he stood up, and he did not know he was going to until he was standing.",
        "He put on his boots. He laced them the way he always laced them, tight across the instep and loose at the top. He put the wallet in his back pocket and the room key in his front pocket and the phone in the pocket of his shirt. He left the folded paper on the dresser. He took the coat down from its hook and put it on, and it settled across his shoulders with its familiar weight, and against his chest, on the left side, he felt the envelope settle too, like a hand laid flat.",
        "Then he went to the window and took the bells down off the nail.",
        "He had not taken them down in four years. The string was looped twice over the nail, and he had to lift it carefully to get it free, and when it came free the bells swung against each other in his hand and did not ring. The string was older than the bells looked. It was not leather and not cord and not any kind of wire he knew. It was dark and fine and very strong, and it had never frayed, and the knot that held its two ends was a knot he had never tied and could not untie. He did not try. He tied the string to his belt on the left side, through the loop above the pocket, and he let the bells hang there against his hip, and he stood for a second with his hand over them, waiting for something. Nothing came. He took his hand away.",
        "Then he opened the wardrobe and reached up to the shelf at the top and took down the long thing wrapped in the moving blanket.",
        "He did not unwrap it. He knew what was in it. He had carried it from room to room for forty years, from town to town, from one wardrobe shelf to the next, and he could not remember buying it, and he could not remember anyone ever giving it to him. It had always simply been one of the things he had. He had never shown it to anyone. When people helped him move, he carried it himself. It was a blade, long and plain, with a hilt wrapped in leather gone black with handling, though he could not remember handling it. That was all. It was not a thing for hanging over a fireplace. It was not a thing for anything, in the life he had.",
        "He unwound the moving blanket and folded it and laid it on the bed. Underneath, the blade was wrapped in a second wrap, of canvas, old, with a strap sewn to it at either end. He had forgotten the strap. He put it over his head and across his chest, from the right shoulder to the left hip, and the blade swung around and settled along his back, and the weight of it went down into his heels, and something in him that had been leaning for a long time stood up straight.",
        "He stood in the middle of the room. He looked at it. The bed, the chair, the hot plate, the sink, the mirror he did not look into. The square of light on the floor. The folded blanket on the bed. He waited to feel something about leaving it, and what he felt was that he had left it a long time ago and was only now catching up.",
        "He locked the door behind him out of habit, and went down the outside stairs one at a time, and the bells did not ring.",
        "* * *",
        "He told himself he was going to the yard early to look the truck over. He told himself that for six blocks. It was the kind of thing he did, and it was a reasonable thing to tell himself, and he walked toward the yard the way he had walked toward it a thousand times, past the houses with their dry lawns and the church with its sign out front, past the shut-up feed store and the place that sold tires, and the bank on the corner, where a sheet of paper was taped inside the glass door that had not been there last week. He did not stop to read it. Nobody on the sidewalk was stopping to read it either. A boy on a bicycle stared at the thing on his back and then pretended not to. A dog came to the end of its chain and did not bark.",
        "At the edge of town the road split.",
        "To the left was the yard. He could see it from there, a quarter mile on, the high chain-link and the rows of trailers and the light poles that would come on at dusk, and his own truck among the others, nose to the fence, dark red under a film of road. To the right was the old county road, the one that had carried everything before the highway came through and carried nothing now. It went straight out into the flat country, two narrow lanes with no line down the middle, the asphalt gone gray and alligatored and heaved up in places where the frost had gotten under it. Grass grew in the cracks. Nobody used it but farm trucks, and not many of those.",
        "He stopped at the place where the road split. He stood there a while.",
