        "“And the sky’s been weird all week.” The clerk looked out the window at it, and his face went young in a different way. “My grandma says it’s been weird. She says the sunsets look wrong. Like they’re coming from the wrong place.” He laughed, a little embarrassed. “She’s like ninety. She says stuff.”",
        "He looked out at the sky. It was a clear evening going toward gold, with a line of haze along the bottom of it and nothing else, and it did not look wrong to him. It did not look like anything. And yet he stood there with the change in his hand and looked at it for so long that the clerk stopped laughing and looked at him instead.",
        "“You okay, man?”",
        "“I’m okay.” He put the change in his pocket. “Tell your grandmother,” he said, and stopped, because he did not know what he had been going to say. The clerk waited. “Tell her she’s right to watch it,” he said finally, and picked up the bottles, and went out.",
        "The bell over the door rang behind him. He did not stop for it this time. But he heard it all the way across the concrete, and for a long time after, out on the road, he kept hearing it, thin and bright and sour, and thinking about the bells at his hip that did not ring, and wondering what the difference was.",
        "He drank the first bottle standing at the edge of the concrete, all of it, without stopping. The water was so cold it hurt his teeth. It went down into him and he felt it go, a cold line through the middle of the heat, and when it was gone he was still thirsty. Not as thirsty. But the thirst was still there underneath, patient, as if it had never really been about water.",
        "He put the second bottle in the deep pocket of the coat. Then the phone in his shirt buzzed against his chest.",
