        "He walked west. The sun was in his eyes and then it was below the cedars and then it was a red line along the bottom of the world, and the whole flat country turned the color of a banked fire.",
        "He walked the way he drove, steadily, not fast, watching the road a long way ahead. The old road went straight for a long time and then made a slow bend to the south to go around a dry pond, and then straightened again. It was badly broken. The frost had heaved it into long low ridges, and in places a whole section had settled and left an edge you could stub a boot on, and in others the asphalt had gone to gravel and the gravel had gone to dust. Grass grew down the middle. Sunflowers, the small wild kind, grew along the edges in a yellow line that went on as far as he could see. Grasshoppers went up out of them ahead of him and rattled away and came down again.",
        "The dust came up behind him with every step. He did not look back at it. He felt it.",
        "A pickup came up from behind him a little before seven. He heard it a long way off, the particular rattle of a loose tailgate and a muffler going bad, and he moved over onto the gravel and kept walking. The pickup slowed as it came up even with him. It was old and pale blue and the passenger window was down, and the man driving it leaned across the bench seat to look out.",
        "“Evening,” the man said.",
        "“Evening.”",
        "The man was older than he was, with a seed cap and a face like a dry creek bed and big hands on the wheel. There was a dog on the seat beside him, a heeler going gray at the muzzle, and the dog looked at him with great seriousness and then looked at the bells on his belt and then back at his face.",
        "“You broke down?”",
