        "He opened his mouth to answer and found there was nothing behind it. Not a town, not a house, not a direction he could have pointed to except the one he was already facing. He closed his mouth again. The man watched him do it and something changed in his face, not suspicion exactly, more like a door being held open a little longer than was polite.",
        "“Out this way,” he said.",
        "“Well, there’s not much out this way.” The man shifted his hands on the wheel. “I’m going as far as the grange hall corner. I can take you that far. Save you an hour.”",
        "“Thank you. I’ll walk.”",
        "“Suit yourself.” The man did not drive on. He looked out past him at the sky in the south, where a long low bank of cloud had begun to show along the very bottom of it, dark blue under the red. “That’s coming,” he said. “Not tonight. But that’s coming. Weekend, they’re saying.” He looked at it a while longer. “Fellow across the road from me hasn’t said a word to me in two years. Over a sign in my yard. Now he’s got a year of canned goods in his barn and a generator, and he waved me down Tuesday to tell me the whole thing’s coming apart. Banks, all of it.” He shook his head. “First thing he’s said to me since. The end of the world.”",
        "“Thursday,” he said. “Thursday night, from the look of it. It’ll stall over the river and sit.”",
        "The man looked at him again, differently. “You farm?”",
